them. What is distributed includes the following.

1. The UCUM unit table (vendor/ucum/ucum-essence.xml)

   The Unified Code for Units of Measure (UCUM), also known as the "UCUM
   Specification," is copyright (c) 1999-2024, Regenstrief Institute, Inc. All
   rights reserved.

   Version 2.2, revision-date 2024-06-17, is reproduced verbatim under the UCUM
   Copyright Notice and License, https://ucum.org/license, and is embedded
   byte-for-byte in the published build. That License grants a worldwide,
   non-exclusive, no-charge, royalty-free, revocable license to reproduce,
   publicly display, publicly perform and distribute the Work, and it permits
   developing and commercializing software that interoperates with UCUM. It does
   not permit using the Work to develop or promulgate a different standard, and it
   does not permit derivative works: the content may not be modified and its
   meaning must remain unchanged. This copy is unmodified, and no derivative of it
   is distributed.

   Disclaimer of warranties: the UCUM Specification is provided "as is" without
   warranty of any kind. See the License at the URL above for the full disclaimer.

   The complete notice ships inside this package at vendor/ucum/NOTICE.md. That
   file, not this summary, is the notice, and it is the copy to read.

2. The UCUM functional-test suite (vendor/ucum/UcumFunctionalTests.xml)

   Copyright (c) 2008-2009 Grahame Grieve and other contributors (Gunther Schadow,
   Lloyd Mackenzie, Vladimir Alexiev), reproduced verbatim under the Eclipse
   Public License v1.0, https://www.eclipse.org/legal/epl-v10.html. It is used
   only as a test fixture in this repository: package.json's "files" does not name
   it, so it is not distributed in the npm package.

3. Code-system identity facts (SYSTEM_IDENTITIES)

   The OID and canonical-URI pairings, names and mnemonics for the code systems
   the resolver recognizes. These identify the systems; no system's code list is
   distributed.

4. SNOMED CT concept identifiers named by the crosswalk resolver

   SNOMED CT identifiers are distributed with their descriptions: the complex-map
   category concepts in MAP_CATEGORIES (447637006, 447638001, 447639009,
   447640006) that a map row's mapCategoryId refers to, and the gender findings
   248152002 (Female) and 248153007 (Male) that a gender IFA rule is written
   against. Further SNOMED CT identifiers, and ICD-10-CM codes, appear in the API
   documentation examples, which are distributed in the compiled declaration
   files (dist/index.d.ts, dist/index.d.cts) and in the build's sourcemaps, whose
   sourcesContent carries every source comment verbatim.
   SNOMED CT is copyright (c) International Health Terminology Standards
   Development Organisation. No SNOMED CT release or refset is distributed; you
   supply the map rows under your own SNOMED CT licence.

5. RxNorm relationship and term-type names (RELA, RELA_INVERSE, TERM_TYPES)

   The drug-graph API is written against RxNorm's own vocabulary, and those names
   are distributed: the relationship names in RELA and RELA_INVERSE
   (has_ingredient, ingredient_of, tradename_of, and the rest), and the term-type
   codes in TERM_TYPES, each with its name as published by the U.S. National
   Library of Medicine ("SCD" reads "Semantic Clinical Drug"). Individual RxNorm
   identifiers also appear in the API documentation examples, with their RxNorm
   names and term types (for instance RXCUI 316151, "lisinopril 10 MG"), and in
   README.md as well as the declaration files and sourcemaps named above. RxNorm
   is produced by the National Library of Medicine. No RxNorm release is
   distributed; you supply it.

Beyond what is named above, no code-system release is distributed with this
package. SNOMED CT, CPT, LOINC, UMLS and RxNorm releases, the CMS GEM files, the
NLM complex-map refset and VSAC value sets are all supplied by the caller, under
whatever terms their steward sets.

